<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/coastal-home.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/coastal-home-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/coastal-home.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li><li aria-current="page">Coast Hurricane Deductibles: The Math Behind a Named Storm Claim</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Claim advice · 7 min read</p><h1>Coast Hurricane Deductibles: The Math Behind a Named Storm Claim</h1><p class="lede">Percentage hurricane deductibles can swallow a Coast claim. See the math for 2% and 5% deductibles, how split policies work, and when the deductible applies.</p></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Mississippi Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-09-14">September 14, 2026</time> · 7 min read</p><h2>Why Coast deductibles look so different</h2><p>Inland, most homeowners know their deductible as a round number: $1,000, maybe $2,500. On and near the Mississippi Coast, many policies switch to a percentage deductible when the damage comes from a hurricane, a named storm or, on some forms, any windstorm. Common percentages run from 2% to 5% of the Coverage A dwelling limit, and on a well-insured house that can mean a five-figure deductible.</p><p>That deductible is applied after the amount of loss is set. Appraisal can change the size of the loss, but it doesn’t change the deductible percentage written on your declarations page. Knowing the math ahead of time helps you see what a dispute is really worth.</p><h2>Running the numbers on 2% and 5%</h2><p>The key point is that a percentage deductible is figured from your Coverage A limit, not from the size of the damage.</p><p><strong>Illustrative example:</strong> Suppose a Gulfport home has a Coverage A limit of $300,000 and a hurricane causes $40,000 in covered wind damage to the dwelling.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Deductible</th><th>Deductible amount</th><th>Paid on a $40,000 loss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flat $2,500</td><td>$2,500</td><td>$37,500</td></tr><tr><td>2% of Coverage A</td><td>$6,000</td><td>$34,000</td></tr><tr><td>5% of Coverage A</td><td>$15,000</td><td>$25,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These figures are illustrative only, and they leave out depreciation, prior payments and other policy terms. They show how the same storm damage nets very different checks depending on the deductible.</p><p>The math also shows why small disputes may not be worth appraising. <strong>Illustrative example:</strong> if the carrier’s estimate on that same home is $14,000 and your contractor says $18,000, a 5% deductible of $15,000 means the carrier pays nothing on its number and $3,000 on yours. Getting the scope right matters most when the loss is well above the deductible.</p><p>Our <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/named-storm-deductible-calculator/">named-storm deductible calculator</a> lets you run your own limits and percentages.</p><div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Check the base of the percentage</b><p>Most forms figure the percentage from Coverage A, but some apply it differently to other structures or contents. Your declarations page and the deductible endorsement show how yours works.</p></div><h2>When the hurricane deductible applies</h2><p>This is where wording matters. Policies define the trigger in different ways, and you should read your own endorsement instead of assuming. Common approaches include:</p><ul><li><strong>Named-storm wording.</strong> The deductible applies to wind damage from a storm that has been named by the National Hurricane Center or National Weather Service, often for a set period around the storm.</li><li><strong>Hurricane wording.</strong> It applies only when a hurricane watch or warning has been issued for part of the state, and for a window of hours after it ends.</li><li><strong>Windstorm or wind-and-hail wording.</strong> Some forms apply the percentage to any wind or hail loss, named storm or not.</li></ul><p>The difference shows up when damage comes from a tropical storm that never reached hurricane strength, or from a tornado spun up by a landfalling system. Under one policy that may be a flat deductible; under another, the percentage. If the carrier applies the larger deductible and you think the trigger wasn’t met, that is a policy interpretation question. Raise it in writing and consider asking a Mississippi attorney.</p><h3>Tropical storms and the storms that follow</h3><p>Coast residents have seen how varied these events can be. Hurricane Nate made landfall near Biloxi as a Category 1 in October 2017. Zeta came ashore in Louisiana in October 2020 but drove strong wind across the Mississippi Coast. Ida in 2021 also landed in Louisiana and still brought down trees in southwest Mississippi. Whether a percentage deductible applied to damage from storms like these depended on each policy’s trigger language, not on where the eye crossed the shoreline. That is why it pays to read the definition before a storm, when you have time to ask your agent questions.</p><h2>How split policies change the picture</h2><p>In Hancock, Harrison, Jackson, Pearl River, Stone and George counties, many owners carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail plus a separate wind policy through the Mississippi Windstorm Underwriting Association. Flood, if insured, sits on a third policy, usually through the NFIP.</p><h3>One storm, more than one deductible</h3><p>With a split setup, each policy applies its own deductible to the damage it covers. A hurricane might trigger the percentage deductible on the MWUA wind policy, the flood deductible on the NFIP policy, and possibly the regular deductible on the homeowners policy for a non-wind item such as a fallen-tree claim that the form treats differently. Each claim is adjusted on its own.</p><h3>Watch for gaps in between</h3><p>When each carrier says the other cause did the damage, owners can end up with deductibles applied on both sides and real damage paid by neither. Keep separate photo sets and estimates for each policy, and read our guide to <a href="/claim-advice/wind-vs-water-after-katrina/">wind vs. water claims</a> for documentation tips.</p><h2>Practical steps before and after the next storm</h2><ul><li>Find your deductible endorsement now, before hurricane season, and note the percentage and trigger.</li><li>Write down your Coverage A limit and do the math so the number isn’t a surprise.</li><li>After a storm, ask the adjuster in writing which deductible was applied and why.</li><li>Compare the deductible to the gap between estimates before deciding whether appraisal is worth it.</li><li>Keep receipts for temporary repairs; mitigation costs often count toward the covered loss.</li></ul><p>If the damage is well above the deductible and the carrier’s estimate looks thin, our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/hurricane-named-storm-appraisal/">hurricane and named-storm appraisal</a> service is built for Coast files. Owners in <a href="/counties-and-cities/biloxi-harrison-county/">Biloxi</a> and across the Coast can reach us the same way as anyone else in the state.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/documenting-tornado-damage/"><small>Guide</small><span>After the Siren: Documenting Tornado Damage in Mississippi</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/ice-storm-tree-claims-north-mississippi/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Storm Tree Claims in North Mississippi: Limits, Roofs and Pipes</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/line-by-line-estimate-reading/"><small>Guide</small><span>Decoding Your Carrier’s Estimate, One Line Item at a Time</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>Free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm CT</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-roadmap/">Appraisal roadmap</a></li><li><a href="/questions-answered/">Questions answered</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">Insurance claim terms</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li></ul></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Free calculators</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/claim-calculator-suite/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">Appraisal Payoff Estimator</a></li><li><a href="/claim-calculator-suite/holdback-recovery-calculator/">Holdback Recovery Calculator</a></li><li><a href="/claim-calculator-suite/roof-pitch-squares-calculator/">Roof Pitch &amp; Squares Calculator</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="band"><div class="wrap band-in rv"><div><h2>Not sure the carrier’s number is right?</h2><p>Send the estimate and a few photos. We’ll review it and tell you what we see — no obligation.</p></div><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
